WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.470 --> 00:00:12.240
MATTHEW O'CONNOR:
Good afternoon.

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:13.240
My name's Matt O'Connor.

00:00:13.240 --> 00:00:16.600
I'm a product manager from
Google's Cloud Platform.

00:00:16.600 --> 00:00:17.870
And this is Aaron Philipp.

00:00:17.870 --> 00:00:19.300
He joins from one
of our partners,

00:00:19.300 --> 00:00:21.740
PricewaterhouseCoopers.

00:00:21.740 --> 00:00:25.850
And we're talking a little
about some cloud security today.

00:00:25.850 --> 00:00:28.900
And we really appreciate
you guys sticking it out,

00:00:28.900 --> 00:00:31.680
because we realize we're the
last stop before cocktails.

00:00:31.680 --> 00:00:35.730
So hopefully we'll make this
interesting for you all.

00:00:35.730 --> 00:00:38.350
Niels this morning spoke
a lot and gave you guys

00:00:38.350 --> 00:00:42.900
a good overview of what
we've done to secure Google

00:00:42.900 --> 00:00:44.061
and to secure the cloud.

00:00:44.061 --> 00:00:46.310
What I was going to talk
about a little bit here today

00:00:46.310 --> 00:00:49.760
is I was going to talk about
our perspective on security

00:00:49.760 --> 00:00:55.020
from a Google point of
view, to hopefully seed

00:00:55.020 --> 00:00:59.464
some thoughts with you all as
you look to taking advantage

00:00:59.464 --> 00:01:02.130
of the cloud and be able to take
advantage of some of the way we

00:01:02.130 --> 00:01:03.110
think about things.

00:01:06.910 --> 00:01:10.070
First of all, we live in
kind of a rough world.

00:01:10.070 --> 00:01:14.900
And so one of the things
that we are constantly doing

00:01:14.900 --> 00:01:18.630
is we're constantly doing a risk
analysis of things that we do.

00:01:18.630 --> 00:01:23.350
And we're assessing
our environment

00:01:23.350 --> 00:01:26.050
to help us make
informed decisions.

00:01:26.050 --> 00:01:27.990
What I'm showing
you here is, this

00:01:27.990 --> 00:01:31.810
is an example of what kind
of the network weather

00:01:31.810 --> 00:01:33.867
looked like back in May.

00:01:33.867 --> 00:01:35.450
This is called the
Digital Attack Map.

00:01:35.450 --> 00:01:37.590
You can go find this
online and look at it.

00:01:37.590 --> 00:01:39.750
And you can see kind of
the weather for today.

00:01:39.750 --> 00:01:42.190
But this is showing
DDoS attacks and stuff,

00:01:42.190 --> 00:01:46.020
and we get a lot of this kind
of traffic on our network.

00:01:46.020 --> 00:01:48.830
And so we've built
capabilities to protect

00:01:48.830 --> 00:01:50.590
a lot of our services.

00:01:50.590 --> 00:01:53.190
Some of our cloud products
take advantage of that.

00:01:53.190 --> 00:01:55.670
For example, if you're using
our App Engine product,

00:01:55.670 --> 00:01:59.390
you're shielded from
these kinds of attacks.

00:01:59.390 --> 00:02:01.270
But I'm just trying
to give you a sense

00:02:01.270 --> 00:02:04.170
of the global environment
that we live in.

00:02:04.170 --> 00:02:06.620
We also think
about, there's kind

00:02:06.620 --> 00:02:08.800
of the traditional
security model

00:02:08.800 --> 00:02:12.174
where we're hard on the
outside, soft on the inside.

00:02:12.174 --> 00:02:13.840
And so one of the
assumptions that we're

00:02:13.840 --> 00:02:15.700
making from a
Google point of view

00:02:15.700 --> 00:02:17.500
is that the network
is compromised

00:02:17.500 --> 00:02:20.510
and that the threat
is close to us.

00:02:20.510 --> 00:02:24.470
And so we're constantly
working on defense in depth

00:02:24.470 --> 00:02:28.960
and doing smart kinds of
things to minimize our risk,

00:02:28.960 --> 00:02:33.290
but also simplify
the way we do things

00:02:33.290 --> 00:02:35.550
to help us mitigate that risk.

00:02:38.200 --> 00:02:42.360
A little bit more on
the threat assessment.

00:02:42.360 --> 00:02:44.170
So I showed you the map.

00:02:44.170 --> 00:02:46.160
I showed you the attacks
of what's going on.

00:02:46.160 --> 00:02:47.535
And these are the
kinds of things

00:02:47.535 --> 00:02:50.700
that we're paying attention to
is to put more into that risk

00:02:50.700 --> 00:02:51.200
model.

00:02:51.200 --> 00:02:53.210
Who are our attackers?

00:02:53.210 --> 00:02:54.960
What kinds of things
are they looking for?

00:02:54.960 --> 00:02:57.620
What are the high value things
that we want to protect?

00:02:57.620 --> 00:03:00.600
And use that to
inform our decisions.

00:03:00.600 --> 00:03:03.130
But the other thing
that we have is

00:03:03.130 --> 00:03:05.690
because we do this on
a very large scale,

00:03:05.690 --> 00:03:07.720
we capture a lot
of data and we use

00:03:07.720 --> 00:03:10.914
that data to help us
make these decisions.

00:03:10.914 --> 00:03:12.830
And I'm going to come
back to that in a minute

00:03:12.830 --> 00:03:15.920
because that is an important
point here in a second.

00:03:19.040 --> 00:03:22.640
Niels talked about the
eight layers of the stack

00:03:22.640 --> 00:03:23.220
this morning.

00:03:23.220 --> 00:03:26.840
And I thought that was
a fabulous presentation.

00:03:26.840 --> 00:03:30.200
Let me talk to you a little
bit about some other kind

00:03:30.200 --> 00:03:32.280
of intangible pieces of that.

00:03:32.280 --> 00:03:36.190
One of the, I wouldn't say
necessarily intangible,

00:03:36.190 --> 00:03:40.410
but one of the things that
we've done and we continue to do

00:03:40.410 --> 00:03:43.600
is we foster a very
strong security culture.

00:03:43.600 --> 00:03:46.630
And we make security
everybody's responsibility.

00:03:46.630 --> 00:03:48.950
We train people in security.

00:03:48.950 --> 00:03:50.810
We partner up with each other.

00:03:50.810 --> 00:03:53.590
When we see common
problems, we'll

00:03:53.590 --> 00:03:57.350
address those and
educate our employees

00:03:57.350 --> 00:04:00.320
to be vigilant for these kinds
of things and avoid stuff.

00:04:00.320 --> 00:04:04.080
And through doing all of
these kinds of activities,

00:04:04.080 --> 00:04:08.190
it helps in simplifying
the problem because we

00:04:08.190 --> 00:04:09.910
have many hands on the problem.

00:04:09.910 --> 00:04:11.340
We've got people
thinking about it

00:04:11.340 --> 00:04:13.520
and we've got people
collaborating with each other

00:04:13.520 --> 00:04:16.139
to reduce these risks and
simplify the problems.

00:04:18.899 --> 00:04:23.780
Part of that culture is also
giving back to the environment

00:04:23.780 --> 00:04:24.770
that we live in.

00:04:24.770 --> 00:04:28.690
And so what I'm showing
you here is just several

00:04:28.690 --> 00:04:31.260
of the innovations that
we've come up with over time

00:04:31.260 --> 00:04:33.660
that we've shared with
the broader community,

00:04:33.660 --> 00:04:37.250
and we hope that you
take advantage of.

00:04:37.250 --> 00:04:40.810
For example, two
factor authentication

00:04:40.810 --> 00:04:44.260
is a big thing that is
really important to us.

00:04:44.260 --> 00:04:48.890
We use that on all
of our employees.

00:04:48.890 --> 00:04:52.432
And it's just the
way we do business.

00:04:52.432 --> 00:04:53.890
And it becomes part
of that culture

00:04:53.890 --> 00:04:54.920
and it becomes a regular thing.

00:04:54.920 --> 00:04:56.670
And it doesn't become
difficult for your employees

00:04:56.670 --> 00:04:58.105
to do to help
protect your assets.

00:05:02.870 --> 00:05:05.370
Niels and Joe talked about
all the infrastructure

00:05:05.370 --> 00:05:07.110
that we built and everything.

00:05:07.110 --> 00:05:11.040
I won't spend a lot
of time on that.

00:05:11.040 --> 00:05:13.370
And then the other piece
of it is that we're

00:05:13.370 --> 00:05:15.620
very operationally vigilant.

00:05:15.620 --> 00:05:19.970
And because we have this
global network, which

00:05:19.970 --> 00:05:23.540
I can show you in my next slide,
we have this point of presence.

00:05:23.540 --> 00:05:26.010
I showed you the
threat map and what's

00:05:26.010 --> 00:05:27.600
happening in our environment.

00:05:27.600 --> 00:05:30.040
And we have this global network.

00:05:30.040 --> 00:05:33.230
And we're in over 33--
we're just basically

00:05:33.230 --> 00:05:37.290
around the globe, which enables
us to deliver applications

00:05:37.290 --> 00:05:40.630
with very high performance
to users all over the planet.

00:05:40.630 --> 00:05:42.390
But also our customers
in Cloud Platform

00:05:42.390 --> 00:05:44.604
get to take advantage
of this network.

00:05:44.604 --> 00:05:46.270
But because there's
networks everywhere,

00:05:46.270 --> 00:05:48.670
we gather a lot of data.

00:05:48.670 --> 00:05:52.840
And the point that
I'm trying to make

00:05:52.840 --> 00:05:55.834
is that we use that to make
data informed decisions.

00:05:55.834 --> 00:05:57.500
And so what I'm about
to do is I'm going

00:05:57.500 --> 00:05:59.370
to hand things over to Aaron.

00:05:59.370 --> 00:06:01.150
And he's going to talk
to you about a tool

00:06:01.150 --> 00:06:05.040
that his team has built
that uses a lot of data

00:06:05.040 --> 00:06:08.030
to help wrangle the
security problem.

00:06:12.503 --> 00:06:15.345
AARON PHILIPP: So thanks Matt.

00:06:15.345 --> 00:06:17.470
Again, just by way of
introduction, my name's Aaron

00:06:17.470 --> 00:06:21.620
Philipp, the head
architect on our solutions

00:06:21.620 --> 00:06:24.070
that we're doing under
the banner of something

00:06:24.070 --> 00:06:26.020
called Reinvent
Security together

00:06:26.020 --> 00:06:27.615
in our Google alliance.

00:06:27.615 --> 00:06:30.220
And just talk just
for two seconds about

00:06:30.220 --> 00:06:34.150
really how this comes
together in practice.

00:06:34.150 --> 00:06:37.950
If you look at PwC, we
have a massive amount

00:06:37.950 --> 00:06:42.010
of business knowledge, how
organizations run globally,

00:06:42.010 --> 00:06:44.804
how they manage risk,
how they address change.

00:06:44.804 --> 00:06:46.970
You look at Google, what
they have from a technology

00:06:46.970 --> 00:06:49.640
standpoint, being able
to take those two things,

00:06:49.640 --> 00:06:54.030
put them together, is something
that as we've gone out

00:06:54.030 --> 00:06:56.640
to our clients and as we've
gone out to the market,

00:06:56.640 --> 00:06:59.360
both from where their
needs sit and then

00:06:59.360 --> 00:07:01.940
also what we've been
able to do to help them

00:07:01.940 --> 00:07:03.190
has been enormously impactful.

00:07:05.980 --> 00:07:07.880
What I'm going to
talk about today,

00:07:07.880 --> 00:07:10.500
and this is kind of a subset
of our security portfolio

00:07:10.500 --> 00:07:12.291
that we're working on
together with Google,

00:07:12.291 --> 00:07:14.750
is what we generally call PwC's
Security Analytics powered

00:07:14.750 --> 00:07:15.920
by Google.

00:07:15.920 --> 00:07:17.770
And there's two main
components to this.

00:07:17.770 --> 00:07:20.514
One is the actual
security analytics itself.

00:07:20.514 --> 00:07:22.930
And the other is something we
call the Threat Intelligence

00:07:22.930 --> 00:07:25.570
Fusion Center, which
allows us to take threat

00:07:25.570 --> 00:07:29.990
intelligence from points all
over the place worldwide,

00:07:29.990 --> 00:07:32.540
fuse it together,
actually understand

00:07:32.540 --> 00:07:34.425
what it means, what
the risks are, and then

00:07:34.425 --> 00:07:35.716
map that into the organization.

00:07:38.480 --> 00:07:41.772
We've actually-- and generally
when I talk about this,

00:07:41.772 --> 00:07:42.980
people are surprised by this.

00:07:42.980 --> 00:07:46.240
But we've actually been doing
this type of work for years,

00:07:46.240 --> 00:07:48.740
for some very,
very large clients.

00:07:48.740 --> 00:07:53.730
We built out really our first
cyber analytics platform

00:07:53.730 --> 00:07:54.619
in 2012.

00:07:54.619 --> 00:07:56.160
And I'll give you
a little background

00:07:56.160 --> 00:07:58.470
on why we had to do this.

00:07:58.470 --> 00:08:02.510
We were working a major
investigation at that point

00:08:02.510 --> 00:08:05.230
where we had something on
the order of 500 servers

00:08:05.230 --> 00:08:06.670
were compromised.

00:08:06.670 --> 00:08:09.300
We had about 550
billion artifacts

00:08:09.300 --> 00:08:12.521
that we had to get through,
many, many, many terabytes

00:08:12.521 --> 00:08:14.020
of data, which at
that point in time

00:08:14.020 --> 00:08:18.160
was a fairly large
scale set of data.

00:08:18.160 --> 00:08:20.880
And we had about
three to four weeks

00:08:20.880 --> 00:08:23.040
to complete the
entire investigation.

00:08:23.040 --> 00:08:24.540
And I'm not sure
how many of you are

00:08:24.540 --> 00:08:26.750
familiar with kind of
traditional forensic techniques

00:08:26.750 --> 00:08:28.770
and those types of
things, but they simply

00:08:28.770 --> 00:08:30.315
were not going to cut it.

00:08:30.315 --> 00:08:32.940
And so we went and we looked and
we said, all right, how can we

00:08:32.940 --> 00:08:34.929
actually accelerate all this?

00:08:34.929 --> 00:08:37.470
And we built out our first
platform, figured out

00:08:37.470 --> 00:08:39.450
how to pull in data
programmatically,

00:08:39.450 --> 00:08:42.179
how to actually look at all
those images at once, how to

00:08:42.179 --> 00:08:44.610
map that from a
normalization standpoint

00:08:44.610 --> 00:08:47.994
into the 550 billion
artifacts we had on top of it,

00:08:47.994 --> 00:08:49.660
and how to actually--
and this is really

00:08:49.660 --> 00:08:51.590
what was most important
for our clients--

00:08:51.590 --> 00:08:54.450
how to map what was happening
in the business on top

00:08:54.450 --> 00:08:57.340
of that technical data.

00:08:57.340 --> 00:09:02.980
And we did all of that in one of
our labs in DC with about a 30

00:09:02.980 --> 00:09:05.820
to 40 node Hadoop cluster.

00:09:05.820 --> 00:09:10.670
So being able to take data
that would have taken us days

00:09:10.670 --> 00:09:14.560
to search using kind
of traditional methods,

00:09:14.560 --> 00:09:17.680
cut that down to something that
we could run through Hadoop

00:09:17.680 --> 00:09:23.050
in about four hours, we thought
we were all now data scientists

00:09:23.050 --> 00:09:25.070
and it was going to
be amazing, right?

00:09:25.070 --> 00:09:26.861
But let me tell you a
little bit about what

00:09:26.861 --> 00:09:29.440
happened at that point.

00:09:29.440 --> 00:09:30.710
We ran our first search.

00:09:30.710 --> 00:09:34.570
And the first search ran on
about 250 terabytes of data.

00:09:34.570 --> 00:09:39.400
And the result set
was five terabytes.

00:09:39.400 --> 00:09:42.800
And we realized very quickly
we had to get much, much better

00:09:42.800 --> 00:09:44.250
from an analytic standpoint.

00:09:44.250 --> 00:09:45.860
Because how we
were doing-- and we

00:09:45.860 --> 00:09:48.530
were state of the art at
that point in time as far

00:09:48.530 --> 00:09:50.500
as Hadoop forensic analysis.

00:09:50.500 --> 00:09:52.790
But what we were
doing simply did not

00:09:52.790 --> 00:09:57.294
work, because the data that
was going in was too big,

00:09:57.294 --> 00:09:59.210
and the data that we
still had to cull through

00:09:59.210 --> 00:10:01.580
after the analytics
was way too big.

00:10:01.580 --> 00:10:04.090
And so we had to really
redesign and look

00:10:04.090 --> 00:10:08.190
at how do we layer in other
things that are nontraditional,

00:10:08.190 --> 00:10:11.500
whether it be information
about the business,

00:10:11.500 --> 00:10:14.110
unstructured data that's
very difficult to work with.

00:10:14.110 --> 00:10:17.200
How do we start hooking
this stuff in, right?

00:10:17.200 --> 00:10:19.790
And we really started
a four year journey

00:10:19.790 --> 00:10:23.710
of a lot of lessons
around just that.

00:10:23.710 --> 00:10:26.780
How do we take in a PDF
that comes from the US

00:10:26.780 --> 00:10:29.250
government that has a series
of technical indicators

00:10:29.250 --> 00:10:31.320
that we need to go look
for and immediately

00:10:31.320 --> 00:10:33.250
operationalize that, right?

00:10:33.250 --> 00:10:36.350
And how do you make sure that
somebody doesn't inadvertently

00:10:36.350 --> 00:10:38.650
type in a keystroke wrong,
and then all of a sudden

00:10:38.650 --> 00:10:40.524
you're firing up all
kinds of false positives

00:10:40.524 --> 00:10:41.960
you didn't know about?

00:10:41.960 --> 00:10:45.260
Being able to solve these
problems at small scale

00:10:45.260 --> 00:10:47.240
is kind of an
interesting problem.

00:10:47.240 --> 00:10:52.000
But being able to solve it at
the scale our clients demand

00:10:52.000 --> 00:10:56.000
from us on a single engagement,
and then what we do globally

00:10:56.000 --> 00:11:00.560
at that scale, is a whole
different ball game.

00:11:00.560 --> 00:11:02.670
And so we built this
stuff out internally.

00:11:02.670 --> 00:11:05.570
And when we started our
alliance with Google,

00:11:05.570 --> 00:11:07.487
we first kind of approached
them and said hey,

00:11:07.487 --> 00:11:09.570
we've got these kind of
interesting security tools

00:11:09.570 --> 00:11:10.640
we're using internally.

00:11:10.640 --> 00:11:14.460
This could be a really
interesting alliance

00:11:14.460 --> 00:11:16.210
where we come together,
use your platform,

00:11:16.210 --> 00:11:17.960
take what we know about
how to actually do

00:11:17.960 --> 00:11:20.630
these types of analytics, how
to layer in the business risk,

00:11:20.630 --> 00:11:24.990
and do it at a real scale.

00:11:24.990 --> 00:11:25.980
And that's what we did.

00:11:25.980 --> 00:11:27.890
And so I just gave
the example of being

00:11:27.890 --> 00:11:31.340
able to take a query from
days to four hours in a Hadoop

00:11:31.340 --> 00:11:32.330
infrastructure.

00:11:32.330 --> 00:11:34.210
We now have that
running essentially

00:11:34.210 --> 00:11:37.130
interactively in a
matter of seconds

00:11:37.130 --> 00:11:39.860
using things like
Google's BigQuery, right?

00:11:39.860 --> 00:11:42.090
And that's really
irregardless of scale.

00:11:42.090 --> 00:11:44.020
I'll talk a little
bit more about this

00:11:44.020 --> 00:11:47.040
when we start talking about
some of the real world stuff

00:11:47.040 --> 00:11:48.030
we deal with.

00:11:48.030 --> 00:11:50.440
But to just give you an idea
of what I consider scale

00:11:50.440 --> 00:11:54.120
and what we consider
scale, we have a client

00:11:54.120 --> 00:11:57.120
that's coming up that
has a requirement for 50

00:11:57.120 --> 00:11:59.410
petabytes of data,
five-zero petabytes of data.

00:11:59.410 --> 00:12:01.950
The proof of concept
we're doing with them

00:12:01.950 --> 00:12:04.770
will encompass about 10 to
15 petabytes worth of data.

00:12:04.770 --> 00:12:06.880
That's what we consider
to be-- I will call

00:12:06.880 --> 00:12:09.620
that moderately large scale.

00:12:09.620 --> 00:12:11.990
There's stuff above it.

00:12:11.990 --> 00:12:15.150
But we have to be able
to address that need,

00:12:15.150 --> 00:12:18.690
because that's what our
clients are asking from us.

00:12:21.680 --> 00:12:24.960
So I have had the
good fortune of being

00:12:24.960 --> 00:12:29.080
able to really kind of go around
the world over the past 12, 18

00:12:29.080 --> 00:12:34.200
months and talk to really some
of the strongest companies

00:12:34.200 --> 00:12:38.370
and strongest public sector
organizations worldwide.

00:12:38.370 --> 00:12:41.140
And this is what has come
out of these conversations.

00:12:41.140 --> 00:12:43.515
When we talk to these guys--
and it's really interesting,

00:12:43.515 --> 00:12:46.070
because irregardless
of sector, irregardless

00:12:46.070 --> 00:12:49.830
of where they're located,
these are the general themes

00:12:49.830 --> 00:12:51.550
that come out.

00:12:51.550 --> 00:12:58.040
Number one, data volume
massively increasing, right?

00:12:58.040 --> 00:13:00.490
This is lead, because
of the problem

00:13:00.490 --> 00:13:03.110
I was mentioning before about
imprecise analytics creating

00:13:03.110 --> 00:13:05.870
a whole bunch of alerts, a
whole bunch of false positives,

00:13:05.870 --> 00:13:08.950
this has led to a massive
backup in the people

00:13:08.950 --> 00:13:11.120
monitoring these alerts, right?

00:13:13.772 --> 00:13:17.600
If you look at any major
breach, the company

00:13:17.600 --> 00:13:20.950
will actually get ripped apart
because someone will invariably

00:13:20.950 --> 00:13:24.534
find an alert in
their security systems

00:13:24.534 --> 00:13:26.700
that would have notified
them about that breach that

00:13:26.700 --> 00:13:27.880
was unaddressed.

00:13:27.880 --> 00:13:32.290
But let's be fair that
organization for half a second.

00:13:32.290 --> 00:13:35.250
It is not uncommon for a
large scale organization today

00:13:35.250 --> 00:13:39.250
to be taking in 10,000
to 15,000 alerts per day.

00:13:39.250 --> 00:13:43.330
Being able to cull that down
from 10,000 to 15,000 alerts

00:13:43.330 --> 00:13:45.650
down to the five
that really matter

00:13:45.650 --> 00:13:52.039
is a massive, massive issue
for companies worldwide.

00:13:52.039 --> 00:13:54.580
The other problem they're having
is because of the large data

00:13:54.580 --> 00:13:57.163
scale and what they're having
to do on the infrastructure side

00:13:57.163 --> 00:14:00.280
to build out
storage, maintain all

00:14:00.280 --> 00:14:02.830
the regulatory
requirements, actually

00:14:02.830 --> 00:14:05.180
be able to look
at the data, they

00:14:05.180 --> 00:14:07.740
are having to make artificial
decisions about what they look

00:14:07.740 --> 00:14:09.564
at and what they don't look at.

00:14:09.564 --> 00:14:10.980
And they're making
these decisions

00:14:10.980 --> 00:14:13.140
not on the value of
the data necessarily,

00:14:13.140 --> 00:14:15.910
but they're making it based
on the size of the data.

00:14:15.910 --> 00:14:19.134
And so what happens is-- and we
see this over and over and over

00:14:19.134 --> 00:14:21.050
again in our incidence
response practice where

00:14:21.050 --> 00:14:24.230
we go in what we call kind of
right of boom after something

00:14:24.230 --> 00:14:28.837
terrible has happened
is, they don't

00:14:28.837 --> 00:14:30.670
have the data necessary
to actually complete

00:14:30.670 --> 00:14:31.690
the full picture.

00:14:31.690 --> 00:14:32.960
And the reason they
don't have that data

00:14:32.960 --> 00:14:35.280
is because they had to make
this kind of artificial choice

00:14:35.280 --> 00:14:36.905
about what they wanted
to keep and what

00:14:36.905 --> 00:14:38.370
they wanted to give away.

00:14:38.370 --> 00:14:41.480
And that really goes
down to this concept

00:14:41.480 --> 00:14:44.240
of limited budget,
insufficient processing power,

00:14:44.240 --> 00:14:45.735
wealth of data not being used.

00:14:45.735 --> 00:14:47.360
And then the last
thing I'll talk about

00:14:47.360 --> 00:14:51.020
is the inability to extract
value out of the data.

00:14:51.020 --> 00:14:53.550
And this is something we've
seen on the private sector side

00:14:53.550 --> 00:14:55.841
and it's also something we've
seen in the public sector

00:14:55.841 --> 00:14:59.100
side where being able to
tell good data from bad

00:14:59.100 --> 00:15:02.230
and actually linking the data
together correctly and showing

00:15:02.230 --> 00:15:04.700
how it joins-- so you may
have something that seems

00:15:04.700 --> 00:15:06.470
worthless as this point
up here, but being

00:15:06.470 --> 00:15:08.531
able to link it with
vulnerabilities,

00:15:08.531 --> 00:15:10.030
be able to with
threat intelligence,

00:15:10.030 --> 00:15:12.405
be able to link it with what's
happening in the business,

00:15:12.405 --> 00:15:15.970
and actually do all of
that at scale and at pace

00:15:15.970 --> 00:15:18.610
is a very, very hard problem.

00:15:18.610 --> 00:15:21.240
So when we looked
at what we wanted

00:15:21.240 --> 00:15:23.350
to address with
this solution, this

00:15:23.350 --> 00:15:26.597
is really where
our heads were at.

00:15:26.597 --> 00:15:28.180
And so we came up
with these four kind

00:15:28.180 --> 00:15:31.040
of foundational requirements.

00:15:31.040 --> 00:15:33.100
One we call
automation detection.

00:15:33.100 --> 00:15:35.680
And I'll explain what
that means in a second.

00:15:35.680 --> 00:15:38.730
Two, superior
intelligence, being

00:15:38.730 --> 00:15:40.760
able to take in
threat intelligence

00:15:40.760 --> 00:15:44.050
from all over the place,
and make best use of it

00:15:44.050 --> 00:15:47.020
at scale-- absolutely critical.

00:15:47.020 --> 00:15:49.020
Visibility of everything,
not just focusing

00:15:49.020 --> 00:15:51.130
on the technical data,
but actually understanding

00:15:51.130 --> 00:15:53.290
what it means to the
business and the organization

00:15:53.290 --> 00:15:56.130
as those alerts come in,
as they are prioritized,

00:15:56.130 --> 00:15:58.480
and as they're dealt with.

00:15:58.480 --> 00:16:00.630
And then lastly,
real time analysis.

00:16:00.630 --> 00:16:03.570
If you're not able to do
one, two, and three at scale,

00:16:03.570 --> 00:16:06.415
in real time-- you
know, there's not

00:16:06.415 --> 00:16:08.540
a whole lot of value in
one, two, and three, right?

00:16:08.540 --> 00:16:10.164
You can do some of
this stuff in batch,

00:16:10.164 --> 00:16:13.220
but being able to do this in
real time and catch this stuff,

00:16:13.220 --> 00:16:16.116
especially as stuff moves
into kind of these newer

00:16:16.116 --> 00:16:18.490
infrastructures where you can
actually automate responses

00:16:18.490 --> 00:16:22.200
in the infrastructure, is
going to be absolutely vital.

00:16:22.200 --> 00:16:25.510
So let's talk about one and
two just for half a second.

00:16:25.510 --> 00:16:28.980
Automation detection, what do
we mean when we talk about this?

00:16:28.980 --> 00:16:33.300
Historically security
analytics, writ large,

00:16:33.300 --> 00:16:36.160
has been about pattern
matching, signature analysis,

00:16:36.160 --> 00:16:37.410
kind of first level.

00:16:37.410 --> 00:16:40.080
first order analysis.

00:16:40.080 --> 00:16:42.500
Some of the more advanced
stuff that's coming out,

00:16:42.500 --> 00:16:44.110
maybe certain
types of clustering

00:16:44.110 --> 00:16:49.530
and those types of things
around basic counting, right?

00:16:49.530 --> 00:16:53.830
It's really interesting
when you see kind of what,

00:16:53.830 --> 00:16:55.950
in these really advanced
clients, what works

00:16:55.950 --> 00:16:59.080
and what doesn't, I can kind
of break it into two buckets

00:16:59.080 --> 00:17:01.220
from what we've seen.

00:17:01.220 --> 00:17:05.089
Things like anomaly detection,
machine learning, those

00:17:05.089 --> 00:17:08.880
are what I would call kind
of near time predictive.

00:17:08.880 --> 00:17:10.819
So maybe they can
predict to an hour.

00:17:10.819 --> 00:17:13.730
Maybe they can break to minutes.

00:17:13.730 --> 00:17:16.569
They're not going to give you
a week warning or two weeks

00:17:16.569 --> 00:17:19.030
warning or three weeks warning
that something's happening.

00:17:19.030 --> 00:17:22.399
Generally they are there to
find the unknown unknowns that

00:17:22.399 --> 00:17:23.940
are already occurring
in the network.

00:17:23.940 --> 00:17:26.750
And there's a massive
amount of research going

00:17:26.750 --> 00:17:28.990
into how to do that better.

00:17:28.990 --> 00:17:32.269
And the great news for us is
having a partner like Google,

00:17:32.269 --> 00:17:34.810
being able to take what they're
doing in the machine learning

00:17:34.810 --> 00:17:38.026
side, leverage that into these
types of business problems

00:17:38.026 --> 00:17:39.650
is actually really,
really interesting.

00:17:39.650 --> 00:17:41.025
I'll talk about
that in a second.

00:17:41.025 --> 00:17:43.590
But when you talk
about long term,

00:17:43.590 --> 00:17:47.940
actually getting ahead of the
threat, threat intelligence,

00:17:47.940 --> 00:17:51.070
by far, is where
that sits today.

00:17:51.070 --> 00:17:54.970
And this is understanding when
a sophisticated threat group has

00:17:54.970 --> 00:17:57.190
started up a new campaign
and understanding

00:17:57.190 --> 00:18:00.000
they're hitting some
other region of the world.

00:18:00.000 --> 00:18:02.280
And we know from past
experience that they start

00:18:02.280 --> 00:18:03.531
there to test all their tools.

00:18:03.531 --> 00:18:05.405
And once they're
comfortable with everything,

00:18:05.405 --> 00:18:07.570
they will then move to
the US, or whatever region

00:18:07.570 --> 00:18:09.790
you happen to be in.

00:18:09.790 --> 00:18:12.682
Having that kind of visibility
and that kind of view,

00:18:12.682 --> 00:18:14.390
and actually being
able to operationalize

00:18:14.390 --> 00:18:19.770
that, is absolutely critical
for organizations going forward.

00:18:19.770 --> 00:18:22.500
So we look at these
number one and number two

00:18:22.500 --> 00:18:25.440
as kind of this near term
and longer term thing.

00:18:25.440 --> 00:18:29.390
The other side of this is
going back to the machine

00:18:29.390 --> 00:18:33.210
learning concepts, being able
to take in some of things

00:18:33.210 --> 00:18:37.180
like the image recognition and
image classifier stuff actually

00:18:37.180 --> 00:18:40.430
can marry into visibility
of everything, right?

00:18:40.430 --> 00:18:45.350
So being able to take
unstructured data that

00:18:45.350 --> 00:18:49.360
are screenshots or desktops,
those types of things,

00:18:49.360 --> 00:18:53.510
and being able to pick up when
a terminal window has been

00:18:53.510 --> 00:18:57.130
opened, just by using
kind of image recognition,

00:18:57.130 --> 00:18:59.910
is something that's very, very
interesting, because there

00:18:59.910 --> 00:19:02.240
may be some obfuscation,
antiforensics they're

00:19:02.240 --> 00:19:06.149
doing on the back end to not
pick up when cmd.exe gets run.

00:19:06.149 --> 00:19:08.190
But we can pick that up
actually on the front end

00:19:08.190 --> 00:19:09.305
using image recognition.

00:19:13.880 --> 00:19:15.774
And I've talked around
this, but I want

00:19:15.774 --> 00:19:16.940
to just hit a couple points.

00:19:16.940 --> 00:19:21.730
And this is, when we talk
about this with our clients,

00:19:21.730 --> 00:19:24.820
being able to kind
of dip into this,

00:19:24.820 --> 00:19:30.037
for lack of a better term,
massive pool of resources,

00:19:30.037 --> 00:19:32.370
whether it be compute, whether
it be storage-- you know,

00:19:32.370 --> 00:19:35.450
I remember when I made the
phone call about the 50 petabyte

00:19:35.450 --> 00:19:40.030
thing, saying hey, is this going
to be a problem for you guys,

00:19:40.030 --> 00:19:41.239
the answer was no.

00:19:41.239 --> 00:19:43.030
And it was not just
no, it was like tell us

00:19:43.030 --> 00:19:44.860
when you want to
start, because we

00:19:44.860 --> 00:19:47.900
can go ahead and just ramp this
up tomorrow if you need to.

00:19:47.900 --> 00:19:50.630
So being able to do
that kind of activity

00:19:50.630 --> 00:19:54.080
and being able to
throw 30, 40 nodes

00:19:54.080 --> 00:19:55.800
if you need to at
just a parsing engine

00:19:55.800 --> 00:19:58.490
because you have some
really complicated ETL

00:19:58.490 --> 00:20:01.700
you're trying to do, and have
that still be cost effective,

00:20:01.700 --> 00:20:05.350
is an absolutely
massive differentiator

00:20:05.350 --> 00:20:08.830
when you look at how
things are done today

00:20:08.830 --> 00:20:12.880
in security versus how they
can be done in the future.

00:20:12.880 --> 00:20:14.980
So having said that, I
want to dive a little bit

00:20:14.980 --> 00:20:16.910
into what we're actually doing
on the threat intelligence

00:20:16.910 --> 00:20:17.410
side.

00:20:20.050 --> 00:20:21.301
We do a lot.

00:20:21.301 --> 00:20:22.800
It's interesting,
people think of us

00:20:22.800 --> 00:20:24.450
as kind of an accounting firm.

00:20:24.450 --> 00:20:27.920
Some of us know that
we have consulting.

00:20:27.920 --> 00:20:29.370
We're actually one
of the largest,

00:20:29.370 --> 00:20:30.800
if not the largest,
information security

00:20:30.800 --> 00:20:33.080
practice in the world and
the consulting [INAUDIBLE].

00:20:33.080 --> 00:20:37.970
And we have offices in, I
think we have 157 countries,

00:20:37.970 --> 00:20:38.740
give or take.

00:20:38.740 --> 00:20:41.930
Don't quote me on
that exact number.

00:20:41.930 --> 00:20:47.000
We have hooks cross region
and around the world.

00:20:47.000 --> 00:20:50.150
And being able to take data
from all those different client

00:20:50.150 --> 00:20:52.750
bases and understand
what that means and map

00:20:52.750 --> 00:20:55.550
that into some of the
other collection activities

00:20:55.550 --> 00:20:57.970
we do is very interesting.

00:20:57.970 --> 00:21:00.340
So we're actually
tracking around 110

00:21:00.340 --> 00:21:03.410
different distinct
threat actors.

00:21:03.410 --> 00:21:05.381
This generates a ton of data.

00:21:05.381 --> 00:21:07.130
So just to talk through
what this actually

00:21:07.130 --> 00:21:13.330
means from a what do you do
with it once you collect it,

00:21:13.330 --> 00:21:17.010
our Threat Intelligence
Fusion Center platform,

00:21:17.010 --> 00:21:19.200
we talked about what
we use internally,

00:21:19.200 --> 00:21:22.980
has something on the order
of, I think it's 200 million,

00:21:22.980 --> 00:21:27.560
give or take, documents in
it, from all over the place.

00:21:27.560 --> 00:21:29.800
We've mapped that into a
very, very large graph.

00:21:29.800 --> 00:21:34.230
And being able to do threat
analytics on that in real time

00:21:34.230 --> 00:21:37.490
as data comes in from all these
different parts of the world,

00:21:37.490 --> 00:21:39.290
absolutely, absolutely critical.

00:21:39.290 --> 00:21:41.460
And when we help our
clients out with how

00:21:41.460 --> 00:21:46.041
they solve these problems, we're
finding more and more and more,

00:21:46.041 --> 00:21:47.790
they start collecting
threat intelligence.

00:21:47.790 --> 00:21:50.480
Maybe they have some
public-private partnership.

00:21:50.480 --> 00:21:53.000
Maybe they have some
feed they're paying for.

00:21:53.000 --> 00:21:56.414
And they realize very
quickly they have a problem,

00:21:56.414 --> 00:21:58.080
because they have,
one, they have no way

00:21:58.080 --> 00:22:00.663
to operationalize it to actually
get it into their environment

00:22:00.663 --> 00:22:02.040
to check what's happening.

00:22:02.040 --> 00:22:04.230
And then two, even if
they did have that way

00:22:04.230 --> 00:22:07.300
to operationalize it, they'd
have no way to rank order it.

00:22:07.300 --> 00:22:09.470
And so what will
invariably happen

00:22:09.470 --> 00:22:15.370
is we'll find out from them that
somehow IP addresses of a CDN

00:22:15.370 --> 00:22:18.720
or some other kind of
massive infrastructure piece

00:22:18.720 --> 00:22:20.530
have ended up on a blacklist.

00:22:20.530 --> 00:22:25.534
And it just knocked out kind of
30% of their business process

00:22:25.534 --> 00:22:27.950
because they inadvertently
blocked something from a threat

00:22:27.950 --> 00:22:30.530
intelligence standpoint without
actually properly vetting

00:22:30.530 --> 00:22:31.860
it first.

00:22:31.860 --> 00:22:34.720
So being able to do
that type of analytics

00:22:34.720 --> 00:22:36.890
as that data comes
in, rank order it,

00:22:36.890 --> 00:22:40.340
combine it together, understand
what a low quality feed is

00:22:40.340 --> 00:22:42.220
telling you versus a
high quality feed, what

00:22:42.220 --> 00:22:44.980
the confidence difference is,
how to make those decisions,

00:22:44.980 --> 00:22:49.750
and how to automate all of that
is something else that's, one,

00:22:49.750 --> 00:22:53.220
for sophisticated clients,
very important now.

00:22:53.220 --> 00:22:55.710
But as the kind of next
year, year and a half,

00:22:55.710 --> 00:22:58.250
two years goes by, as more
threat intelligence comes out,

00:22:58.250 --> 00:23:00.070
especially in the US around
some of the laws that have just

00:23:00.070 --> 00:23:02.340
been released, is going
to be absolutely vital.

00:23:05.640 --> 00:23:09.660
The other side of that too
is information sharing.

00:23:09.660 --> 00:23:13.720
So how does that get linked
with companies together?

00:23:13.720 --> 00:23:15.170
So if you have a
set of indicators

00:23:15.170 --> 00:23:17.336
that you have gotten off
your network because you're

00:23:17.336 --> 00:23:19.529
seeing some campaign that
no one else is seeing,

00:23:19.529 --> 00:23:21.070
how do you make sure
that efficiently

00:23:21.070 --> 00:23:26.690
moves across the wire to all
of your partners, to folks

00:23:26.690 --> 00:23:29.420
that you have information
sharing agreements with?

00:23:29.420 --> 00:23:31.980
And how do you make sure
that that doesn't somehow

00:23:31.980 --> 00:23:35.800
have a knock on effect to
where you adversely disrupt

00:23:35.800 --> 00:23:40.190
the business of a partner?

00:23:40.190 --> 00:23:42.970
When you start kind of unpeeling
what these threat intelligence

00:23:42.970 --> 00:23:45.050
concepts actually
mean, you start

00:23:45.050 --> 00:23:47.910
to see what the challenges
from an analytic standpoint

00:23:47.910 --> 00:23:49.550
are really starting to present.

00:23:49.550 --> 00:23:52.220
So it's not just
about having pictures

00:23:52.220 --> 00:23:53.920
of the building
these guys work in,

00:23:53.920 --> 00:23:57.136
which is kind of
academically interesting.

00:23:57.136 --> 00:23:58.510
But it's really
about, all right,

00:23:58.510 --> 00:24:00.810
how do I use this
information to best protect

00:24:00.810 --> 00:24:05.190
my estate, my business
partner's estate, and kind

00:24:05.190 --> 00:24:07.190
of the ecosystem writ large.

00:24:11.120 --> 00:24:13.530
So I'll talk a little bit
about what we actually

00:24:13.530 --> 00:24:16.980
do with our Threat
Intelligence Fusion Center.

00:24:16.980 --> 00:24:20.565
This was born out of our
internal practitioners wanting

00:24:20.565 --> 00:24:21.856
to share data more effectively.

00:24:24.500 --> 00:24:29.080
And so we had to build a
platform that would allow them

00:24:29.080 --> 00:24:34.900
to very easily, seamlessly, and
frictionlessly upload these PDF

00:24:34.900 --> 00:24:38.320
reports, Word documents,
text files, emails,

00:24:38.320 --> 00:24:41.490
whatever it may be, drag and
drop them in, extract out

00:24:41.490 --> 00:24:44.800
all the indicators, and
then actually operationalize

00:24:44.800 --> 00:24:46.310
that data.

00:24:46.310 --> 00:24:48.230
So there was a
massive amount of work

00:24:48.230 --> 00:24:51.930
that went into figuring out
how to, when you drag and drop

00:24:51.930 --> 00:24:54.580
a PDF in, how to
rip out kind of all

00:24:54.580 --> 00:24:56.600
the technical data
in that report,

00:24:56.600 --> 00:24:58.110
and then create
a workflow around

00:24:58.110 --> 00:25:01.520
that so you have second and
third level reviewers actually

00:25:01.520 --> 00:25:03.500
saying yes, this is
what we think it is.

00:25:03.500 --> 00:25:05.346
Yes, we're assigning
this confidence to it.

00:25:05.346 --> 00:25:07.220
Yes, we think it's this
malicious, et cetera,

00:25:07.220 --> 00:25:08.497
et cetera, et cetera.

00:25:08.497 --> 00:25:10.330
Because when you start
getting at the scales

00:25:10.330 --> 00:25:11.910
that we operate
at just internally

00:25:11.910 --> 00:25:17.750
and also our clients
operate at, 95% accuracy

00:25:17.750 --> 00:25:19.280
is not good enough
because that can

00:25:19.280 --> 00:25:22.750
mean massive amounts of massive
amounts of false positives

00:25:22.750 --> 00:25:24.600
and massive amounts of hits.

00:25:24.600 --> 00:25:27.570
So there is still
very much still

00:25:27.570 --> 00:25:31.755
a role for kind of this process
wrapped around the technology.

00:25:34.680 --> 00:25:38.869
Being able to integrate with
third party solutions that

00:25:38.869 --> 00:25:40.660
actually allow us to
go out and interrogate

00:25:40.660 --> 00:25:47.040
the infrastructure-- so hooking
into a solution such as Tanium

00:25:47.040 --> 00:25:49.250
or one of these
endpoint solutions

00:25:49.250 --> 00:25:51.241
is absolutely
critical, because those

00:25:51.241 --> 00:25:52.740
are the tools that
are going to have

00:25:52.740 --> 00:25:55.198
the visibility at the endpoints
to be able to push and pull

00:25:55.198 --> 00:25:59.940
this data and actually allow
us to pivot very, very quickly.

00:25:59.940 --> 00:26:04.550
When we look at kind of
the life of a SOC analyst,

00:26:04.550 --> 00:26:06.270
security operations
center analyst,

00:26:06.270 --> 00:26:08.300
and kind of the SOC
1.0 architecture

00:26:08.300 --> 00:26:13.190
which exists today, they spend
hours switching between tools,

00:26:13.190 --> 00:26:15.220
switching between
technologies, switching

00:26:15.220 --> 00:26:17.985
between online-offline
stuff, going out

00:26:17.985 --> 00:26:21.170
and investigating things, trying
to pull all this data together.

00:26:21.170 --> 00:26:24.204
Typically they jump into some
kind of Excel spreadsheet.

00:26:24.204 --> 00:26:25.620
They create some
kind of timeline.

00:26:25.620 --> 00:26:27.286
It's not uncommon for
these SOCs to have

00:26:27.286 --> 00:26:29.701
one guy whose entire
job it is to maintain

00:26:29.701 --> 00:26:31.200
some kind of
spreadsheet of metrics.

00:26:34.210 --> 00:26:36.280
If you take that
and actually move

00:26:36.280 --> 00:26:38.840
to kind of this integrated
architecture concept

00:26:38.840 --> 00:26:41.460
where all the threat
intelligence is married

00:26:41.460 --> 00:26:43.760
to all of the endpoint
technologies, which

00:26:43.760 --> 00:26:47.822
has a massive analytics backbone
behind it that actually allows

00:26:47.822 --> 00:26:49.780
you to overlay the business
risk on top of that

00:26:49.780 --> 00:26:51.880
and see it all as kind
of one pane of glass

00:26:51.880 --> 00:26:54.290
as part of the native
alert, what we're

00:26:54.290 --> 00:26:57.450
seeing in terms of
operational efficiency

00:26:57.450 --> 00:26:59.120
is just absolutely massive.

00:26:59.120 --> 00:27:03.300
So they're going
from hours to days

00:27:03.300 --> 00:27:07.960
to investigate an incident
down to half hour, 15 minutes,

00:27:07.960 --> 00:27:11.000
eight minutes in some cases.

00:27:11.000 --> 00:27:14.680
And so there's a lot to be said
for doing this work upfront

00:27:14.680 --> 00:27:17.780
and actually setting
this single pane of glass

00:27:17.780 --> 00:27:20.130
up and actually pulling all
the data into one place.

00:27:20.130 --> 00:27:23.620
We went, just on the forensic
side and the IR side,

00:27:23.620 --> 00:27:28.270
by doing that, our average
time to investigate a system

00:27:28.270 --> 00:27:31.330
went from about
three to four days

00:27:31.330 --> 00:27:33.930
down to an hour,
hour and a half.

00:27:33.930 --> 00:27:36.170
And that's doing kind of
the full forensic analysis.

00:27:36.170 --> 00:27:37.670
Because we automated
on the back end

00:27:37.670 --> 00:27:40.050
and we parallelized on the
back end all the extraction

00:27:40.050 --> 00:27:42.120
of that data out.

00:27:42.120 --> 00:27:45.860
And we automated what we
could and we left the art

00:27:45.860 --> 00:27:48.010
to the analysts to
be able to construct

00:27:48.010 --> 00:27:49.910
what they need to construct.

00:27:49.910 --> 00:27:52.544
It's my personal
opinion, we're going

00:27:52.544 --> 00:27:53.960
to see these kind
of optimizations

00:27:53.960 --> 00:27:54.850
all over the place.

00:27:54.850 --> 00:27:55.945
And so when we talk
about machine learning

00:27:55.945 --> 00:27:57.790
and those type of things, those
things are very interesting.

00:27:57.790 --> 00:28:00.430
But just being able to
get the data in one place

00:28:00.430 --> 00:28:03.350
and have all the data
present for analysis

00:28:03.350 --> 00:28:08.971
is actually going to be huge in
getting efficiencies out of it.

00:28:08.971 --> 00:28:11.470
So I want to talk a little bit
about what a proof of concept

00:28:11.470 --> 00:28:12.630
looks like for us.

00:28:12.630 --> 00:28:15.560
These data numbers
are a little small

00:28:15.560 --> 00:28:19.120
compared to what we're actually
doing now on proof of concepts.

00:28:19.120 --> 00:28:23.050
If you look at what
we're doing currently,

00:28:23.050 --> 00:28:25.340
we actually have
a proof of concept

00:28:25.340 --> 00:28:28.720
where we're taking about
six terabytes of data a day.

00:28:28.720 --> 00:28:31.500
We're maintaining that 30
millisecond processing time.

00:28:31.500 --> 00:28:32.920
So that's everything
from ETL all

00:28:32.920 --> 00:28:35.910
the way through to analytics,
threat intelligence look up,

00:28:35.910 --> 00:28:37.539
et cetera.

00:28:37.539 --> 00:28:40.080
Instead of 2 million queries--
which is still a great number.

00:28:40.080 --> 00:28:41.570
I'll talk about
that in a second--

00:28:41.570 --> 00:28:44.740
we're actually seeing about
45, 50 million queries

00:28:44.740 --> 00:28:46.009
being run against that data.

00:28:46.009 --> 00:28:48.550
So the more you get the data,
the more you make it accessible

00:28:48.550 --> 00:28:51.620
and the easier you
make it to query,

00:28:51.620 --> 00:28:53.800
ironically we're finding
people query it more.

00:28:53.800 --> 00:28:55.820
Which is great.

00:28:55.820 --> 00:28:59.870
So let's talk a little bit
about this proof of concept.

00:28:59.870 --> 00:29:07.377
So we started out with I think
there were 32 use cases that

00:29:07.377 --> 00:29:09.710
were going to be completely
custom built for the client.

00:29:12.450 --> 00:29:14.250
We started taking in data.

00:29:14.250 --> 00:29:18.262
We took data from about
400 different log types.

00:29:18.262 --> 00:29:20.220
We took it in as just
kind of a massive stream.

00:29:20.220 --> 00:29:24.060
We had them mirror off their
existing log infrastructure.

00:29:24.060 --> 00:29:29.060
Within about 36, 48
hours, most of that

00:29:29.060 --> 00:29:32.010
ended up having to be
a bit custom parsing.

00:29:32.010 --> 00:29:35.092
36, 48 hours, we had 96%
of the data parsed out.

00:29:35.092 --> 00:29:36.800
I think in the next
week we had something

00:29:36.800 --> 00:29:38.770
in the area of 99.5% parsed out.

00:29:38.770 --> 00:29:43.430
And then we got the last
0.5% when we needed it.

00:29:43.430 --> 00:29:46.670
So being able to have
that kind of agility

00:29:46.670 --> 00:29:49.350
and being able to scale up,
just transparently what you're

00:29:49.350 --> 00:29:51.420
doing just on the
parsing side and actually

00:29:51.420 --> 00:29:56.120
make that data usable
that quickly, was massive.

00:29:56.120 --> 00:29:59.240
Because we started receiving
data on a Thursday.

00:29:59.240 --> 00:30:00.860
And then the
following Friday, we

00:30:00.860 --> 00:30:03.890
were down there with
15 custom use cases

00:30:03.890 --> 00:30:06.210
fully executed through,
and showing them

00:30:06.210 --> 00:30:08.416
stuff that none of
their other security

00:30:08.416 --> 00:30:09.790
tools or other
security platforms

00:30:09.790 --> 00:30:12.090
had been able to show them.

00:30:12.090 --> 00:30:14.090
And a lot of that goes
back to how quickly

00:30:14.090 --> 00:30:16.340
we were able to spin up the
Google infrastructure, how

00:30:16.340 --> 00:30:18.090
we were able to use
those tools, how

00:30:18.090 --> 00:30:23.090
we are able to not have to focus
so much on absolute efficiency

00:30:23.090 --> 00:30:25.240
of the code, but actually
just getting it to work,

00:30:25.240 --> 00:30:28.540
and then able to cut to make it
more efficient on the back end

00:30:28.540 --> 00:30:31.090
as we could scale it down.

00:30:31.090 --> 00:30:34.050
Really interesting story kind
of about the 2 million queries

00:30:34.050 --> 00:30:39.290
run-- 1 million of those were
run within a 24 hour period.

00:30:39.290 --> 00:30:45.776
That kind of a spike, on a
lot of on-prem platforms where

00:30:45.776 --> 00:30:48.400
they just have a couple servers
running and they're running it,

00:30:48.400 --> 00:30:50.540
would have absolutely
tipped the platform over,

00:30:50.540 --> 00:30:52.240
would have just crashed it.

00:30:52.240 --> 00:30:54.490
The only notice we had that
something was a little bit

00:30:54.490 --> 00:30:58.019
up was we got an email
saying hey, can you

00:30:58.019 --> 00:30:58.810
check connectivity.

00:30:58.810 --> 00:31:01.660
It looks like the platform may
be running a little bit slow.

00:31:01.660 --> 00:31:03.620
And actually what
happened is we kind of hit

00:31:03.620 --> 00:31:04.960
the limits on the scaling.

00:31:04.960 --> 00:31:09.900
We upped the limits
within, I think, two hours.

00:31:09.900 --> 00:31:11.850
No downtime, no anything else.

00:31:11.850 --> 00:31:13.777
The entire platform
was back up to normal.

00:31:13.777 --> 00:31:16.360
Another really interesting thing
that we found out about this,

00:31:16.360 --> 00:31:18.901
and we actually found about it
after the fact-- I didn't even

00:31:18.901 --> 00:31:23.330
know this until the CSO
told somebody else about it.

00:31:23.330 --> 00:31:25.654
They decided to do a stress
test on the infrastructure

00:31:25.654 --> 00:31:27.820
and find out if they could
crash our infrastructure,

00:31:27.820 --> 00:31:30.380
because they wanted to
see what our limits were.

00:31:30.380 --> 00:31:32.960
And so they went ahead and
they just, without telling us,

00:31:32.960 --> 00:31:36.140
they tripled the amount
of data coming in.

00:31:36.140 --> 00:31:38.350
And we didn't even notice.

00:31:38.350 --> 00:31:44.230
All of the speeds, everything
else kind of scaled up.

00:31:44.230 --> 00:31:46.690
It all kept running,
no problems.

00:31:46.690 --> 00:31:50.090
Being able to do that because
of what Google's put together

00:31:50.090 --> 00:31:51.770
and their infrastructure
and what we're

00:31:51.770 --> 00:31:54.000
doing in these kind of
cloud native technologies,

00:31:54.000 --> 00:31:56.220
you try that with an
on-prem-- and they did try it

00:31:56.220 --> 00:32:00.230
with an on-prem technology,
and it crashed and fell over

00:32:00.230 --> 00:32:05.140
and took the other group a
couple days to actually get

00:32:05.140 --> 00:32:07.110
back to operational form.

00:32:10.050 --> 00:32:14.620
And this story occurs
not just at small scale,

00:32:14.620 --> 00:32:17.010
but large scale, which is great.

00:32:17.010 --> 00:32:19.974
So when we actually look
at what we're doing here,

00:32:19.974 --> 00:32:21.390
every piece of
threat intelligence

00:32:21.390 --> 00:32:23.681
that sits in our threat
intelligence portal, the Threat

00:32:23.681 --> 00:32:26.350
Intelligence Fusion Center,
is looked up in real time

00:32:26.350 --> 00:32:28.420
as that data comes through.

00:32:28.420 --> 00:32:30.070
So for our internal
instance, that's

00:32:30.070 --> 00:32:36.500
about 22 million indicators,
massive number of indicators.

00:32:36.500 --> 00:32:39.857
For clients it's generally
a bit smaller number.

00:32:39.857 --> 00:32:41.940
But it gives you an idea
of what you're able to do

00:32:41.940 --> 00:32:43.273
and actually able to rank order.

00:32:43.273 --> 00:32:47.650
And this all results
in being able to take

00:32:47.650 --> 00:32:50.334
in multiple
dimensions so you can

00:32:50.334 --> 00:32:51.500
look at threat intelligence.

00:32:51.500 --> 00:32:53.160
If you have a hit,
you can marry it

00:32:53.160 --> 00:32:55.670
to what's actually
on the system.

00:32:55.670 --> 00:32:57.370
So what the business
risk of it is?

00:32:57.370 --> 00:32:58.830
Does it contain
third party data?

00:32:58.830 --> 00:33:00.205
Are there certain
vulnerabilities

00:33:00.205 --> 00:33:02.100
that we know about
that are on the system?

00:33:02.100 --> 00:33:03.730
Does it play a
very important role

00:33:03.730 --> 00:33:05.450
in certain types of
business processes?

00:33:05.450 --> 00:33:07.730
And how does all of
that link together?

00:33:07.730 --> 00:33:09.630
And being able to use
that to rank order

00:33:09.630 --> 00:33:14.550
the criticality of the alert so
that you can start pushing down

00:33:14.550 --> 00:33:17.520
some of the noise alerts
and actually using

00:33:17.520 --> 00:33:20.230
some kind of interesting stuff
to fully marry those out,

00:33:20.230 --> 00:33:22.199
and then really
raising to the top

00:33:22.199 --> 00:33:24.490
the ones that are going to
impact the business in very,

00:33:24.490 --> 00:33:27.170
very critical ways.

00:33:27.170 --> 00:33:31.020
And so just the punchline
on this proof of concept

00:33:31.020 --> 00:33:38.250
is we were side by side with
another company at the time.

00:33:38.250 --> 00:33:40.040
In the time it took
them to, I think,

00:33:40.040 --> 00:33:43.640
execute five or six
custom use cases,

00:33:43.640 --> 00:33:47.000
we were actually able
to execute on 38.

00:33:47.000 --> 00:33:49.309
And it was so much so that
they actually asked us--

00:33:49.309 --> 00:33:51.850
and this is something really,
really interesting as well when

00:33:51.850 --> 00:33:54.290
you use these types
of cloud technologies

00:33:54.290 --> 00:33:56.660
where you have this
open data architecture.

00:33:56.660 --> 00:33:58.972
The fraud team found out
about this and said hey,

00:33:58.972 --> 00:34:01.430
we know you're doing a bunch
of stuff on the security side.

00:34:01.430 --> 00:34:03.660
But you have that
data sitting there.

00:34:03.660 --> 00:34:05.900
Can you do that data for fraud?

00:34:05.900 --> 00:34:09.480
And within I think
a day or two, we

00:34:09.480 --> 00:34:11.080
had a couple
dashboards put together

00:34:11.080 --> 00:34:14.230
showing how we could use
all the same sets of data,

00:34:14.230 --> 00:34:16.889
with a little bit of additional
stuff from a transaction

00:34:16.889 --> 00:34:19.190
standpoint, financial
transaction standpoint,

00:34:19.190 --> 00:34:21.320
to show how we can now
take that same data

00:34:21.320 --> 00:34:24.462
and have a fraud
alerting system.

00:34:24.462 --> 00:34:26.670
And they're looking at this
now saying wait a minute,

00:34:26.670 --> 00:34:29.440
we have all these
different infrastructures

00:34:29.440 --> 00:34:32.219
in our environment that are
collecting this type of data.

00:34:32.219 --> 00:34:34.270
And we have many different
copies of this data.

00:34:34.270 --> 00:34:36.580
Why don't we just have
it all in one place,

00:34:36.580 --> 00:34:39.010
and then all these
different groups can use it.

00:34:39.010 --> 00:34:41.379
So we're now seeing
the applications group,

00:34:41.379 --> 00:34:43.670
the infrastructure group,
the security group, the fraud

00:34:43.670 --> 00:34:46.550
group, some of the customer
satisfaction groups,

00:34:46.550 --> 00:34:48.370
are all looking at
how they can use

00:34:48.370 --> 00:34:51.624
the same data with different
cuts in different ways.

00:34:51.624 --> 00:34:53.290
And that's when you
really, really start

00:34:53.290 --> 00:34:55.010
to get to very
interesting things,

00:34:55.010 --> 00:34:57.464
because then you're
able to, if you

00:34:57.464 --> 00:34:59.880
see something on the customer
satisfaction side-- somebody

00:34:59.880 --> 00:35:01.410
calls into a call center.

00:35:01.410 --> 00:35:03.160
They're trying to
social engineer.

00:35:03.160 --> 00:35:05.370
And you want to marry that
up to what's happening

00:35:05.370 --> 00:35:07.280
on a database at the same time.

00:35:07.280 --> 00:35:09.990
That becomes very easy
because all the data is just

00:35:09.990 --> 00:35:10.879
sitting there.

00:35:10.879 --> 00:35:13.420
And you can start to do these
really, really interesting kind

00:35:13.420 --> 00:35:16.160
of data driven analytics.

00:35:16.160 --> 00:35:20.310
And I'll go ahead, and I just
have a couple screenshots here

00:35:20.310 --> 00:35:23.570
just to talk through.

00:35:23.570 --> 00:35:26.090
This one's what I would
call fairly basic,

00:35:26.090 --> 00:35:29.910
but just to show how we're
able to put a lot of this

00:35:29.910 --> 00:35:32.790
on one pane of glass where you
have stuff coming in from all

00:35:32.790 --> 00:35:35.200
over the place, all these
different devices, and then

00:35:35.200 --> 00:35:39.110
also actually showing the
type of data that resides

00:35:39.110 --> 00:35:41.610
on these different systems.

00:35:41.610 --> 00:35:44.020
And being able to
link all this up just

00:35:44.020 --> 00:35:48.380
is part of the native platform
and something our clients have

00:35:48.380 --> 00:35:51.520
found to be enormously useful.

00:35:51.520 --> 00:35:55.980
Anomaly detection-- we
have anomaly detection.

00:35:55.980 --> 00:35:59.510
Just to walk through, this
is a very basic example

00:35:59.510 --> 00:36:01.870
of how we would do this
with firewall data.

00:36:01.870 --> 00:36:03.620
We're actually able
to use with user data,

00:36:03.620 --> 00:36:06.276
we have a portfolio of
five different algorithms

00:36:06.276 --> 00:36:07.650
that we use for
different things,

00:36:07.650 --> 00:36:09.690
depending on what
the situation is.

00:36:12.940 --> 00:36:16.920
And we can run anomaly detection
kind of on your basic stuff.

00:36:16.920 --> 00:36:20.120
But then actually when you start
to get to user activity, data

00:36:20.120 --> 00:36:21.640
activity, all those
types of things

00:36:21.640 --> 00:36:23.350
where you start to get some
really, really interesting

00:36:23.350 --> 00:36:24.960
use cases on the
anomaly detection.

00:36:24.960 --> 00:36:27.940
And this is really all about
driving to the unknown unknowns

00:36:27.940 --> 00:36:29.167
that you may not know about.

00:36:29.167 --> 00:36:31.000
And I don't know how
many of you have worked

00:36:31.000 --> 00:36:35.740
with anomaly detection
before, but it

00:36:35.740 --> 00:36:39.987
can be very messy to deal with
because when an anomaly fires,

00:36:39.987 --> 00:36:41.820
there's generally very
little context of why

00:36:41.820 --> 00:36:43.740
that anomaly fired
other than to say,

00:36:43.740 --> 00:36:46.690
this is what we're looking
at, and we found an anomaly.

00:36:46.690 --> 00:36:49.600
So being able to have all
this data in one pane of glass

00:36:49.600 --> 00:36:51.890
to where you can drill
in very quickly to all

00:36:51.890 --> 00:36:54.910
the data around the anomaly and
understand what was happening,

00:36:54.910 --> 00:36:58.500
both with the event
itself but also

00:36:58.500 --> 00:37:01.370
at the baseline understanding
how it typically looks

00:37:01.370 --> 00:37:04.545
is something really,
really critical.

00:37:04.545 --> 00:37:06.490
And I have talked about
this already a bit,

00:37:06.490 --> 00:37:09.314
but full threat
intel integration.

00:37:09.314 --> 00:37:10.980
This was something
very important to us.

00:37:10.980 --> 00:37:14.390
So it's not about having two
separate systems, one that

00:37:14.390 --> 00:37:16.627
manages threat intelligence
and then one that manages

00:37:16.627 --> 00:37:18.460
the lookups against the
threat intelligence,

00:37:18.460 --> 00:37:21.100
but actually having all
of that connected together

00:37:21.100 --> 00:37:22.690
and being able to
act on that data

00:37:22.690 --> 00:37:26.030
in some kind of algorithmic
way, really, really critical.

00:37:26.030 --> 00:37:28.630
Because we see time
and time and time again

00:37:28.630 --> 00:37:31.070
from a process standpoint,
when an organization

00:37:31.070 --> 00:37:34.280
has those two
systems disconnected,

00:37:34.280 --> 00:37:36.550
threat intelligence get
missed, because they

00:37:36.550 --> 00:37:38.760
invariably for some reason
will not move that over.

00:37:38.760 --> 00:37:41.300
It moves over in batches.

00:37:41.300 --> 00:37:44.300
The other side of that too is
when you introduce new threat

00:37:44.300 --> 00:37:45.740
intelligence into
the ecosystem--

00:37:45.740 --> 00:37:49.220
so you have some new report
that just came out from ICS-CERT

00:37:49.220 --> 00:37:54.080
or whatever it may be-- being
able to do that retrospectively

00:37:54.080 --> 00:37:56.350
and say automatically
every 24 hours,

00:37:56.350 --> 00:37:59.080
go back and search my logs
for all of these indicators

00:37:59.080 --> 00:38:01.680
and tell me if I have
an issue, and then

00:38:01.680 --> 00:38:04.580
have a report sent out
nightly on that is something

00:38:04.580 --> 00:38:07.270
really critical as well.

00:38:07.270 --> 00:38:09.717
In the second screen shot
you'll see down here,

00:38:09.717 --> 00:38:11.300
you see kind of the
beginnings of what

00:38:11.300 --> 00:38:13.869
looks like a network graph.

00:38:13.869 --> 00:38:16.410
The other thing that we see as
being really, really important

00:38:16.410 --> 00:38:18.860
is being able to
actually keep all of this

00:38:18.860 --> 00:38:20.904
in one related structure.

00:38:20.904 --> 00:38:23.070
Because you will start to
see kind of these emergent

00:38:23.070 --> 00:38:26.090
patterns pop out when
you do it that way.

00:38:26.090 --> 00:38:28.760
And if you're using some of
these smaller scale graph

00:38:28.760 --> 00:38:32.090
tools, which are phenomenal
tools in their own right,

00:38:32.090 --> 00:38:34.504
and you kind of have
these different versions

00:38:34.504 --> 00:38:36.670
of each threat group or
different versions of what's

00:38:36.670 --> 00:38:39.670
happening, you are going
to miss connections

00:38:39.670 --> 00:38:43.980
between threat groups,
between tools usage,

00:38:43.980 --> 00:38:44.950
between infrastructure.

00:38:44.950 --> 00:38:48.661
So to make this concrete,
we see time and time again

00:38:48.661 --> 00:38:50.910
where an individual has
defected from one threat group

00:38:50.910 --> 00:38:53.670
to another, especially in some
of the cyber crime groups.

00:38:53.670 --> 00:38:55.740
And also the cyber
crime group picks up

00:38:55.740 --> 00:39:00.210
a whole new range of tactics
that they never used before.

00:39:00.210 --> 00:39:02.300
Being able to pick
that up and to actually

00:39:02.300 --> 00:39:04.630
understand what's happening
underneath the hood there,

00:39:04.630 --> 00:39:07.030
and the fact that somebody
may have moved over,

00:39:07.030 --> 00:39:09.440
and also now whenever you
go up against group X,

00:39:09.440 --> 00:39:11.314
you need to start looking
for all the attacks

00:39:11.314 --> 00:39:13.580
that group Y uses as
well is enormously

00:39:13.580 --> 00:39:17.710
beneficial to our clients and
protecting kind of society writ

00:39:17.710 --> 00:39:19.730
large.

00:39:19.730 --> 00:39:22.820
Being able to do that
without all that data sitting

00:39:22.820 --> 00:39:25.500
in one place is a very,
very difficult exercise.

00:39:25.500 --> 00:39:29.100
So it's absolutely critical
to keep very, very noisy data

00:39:29.100 --> 00:39:31.320
at scale and then understand
how to kind of rank

00:39:31.320 --> 00:39:32.620
order that data up and down.

00:39:35.750 --> 00:39:39.230
So endpoint
monitoring-- and there's

00:39:39.230 --> 00:39:42.050
a kind of massive conversation
going on the industry

00:39:42.050 --> 00:39:45.080
now about agent versus no agent,
when do you deploy an agent,

00:39:45.080 --> 00:39:46.554
when do you not need an agent.

00:39:46.554 --> 00:39:47.720
And it's really fascinating.

00:39:47.720 --> 00:39:52.450
So this entire dashboard was
created with no endpoint,

00:39:52.450 --> 00:39:56.610
with actually no
agent on the servers.

00:39:56.610 --> 00:39:58.839
We were actually just
using the native logs that

00:39:58.839 --> 00:40:00.130
came off the server to do this.

00:40:03.130 --> 00:40:04.510
Typically it's
actually very hard

00:40:04.510 --> 00:40:06.551
to do these types of things
because a lot of this

00:40:06.551 --> 00:40:09.324
came out of a certain type
of event log type that's

00:40:09.324 --> 00:40:11.740
very, very unstructured, very
difficult to deal with, very

00:40:11.740 --> 00:40:13.630
hard to parse.

00:40:13.630 --> 00:40:16.770
Being able to use
Google's platform

00:40:16.770 --> 00:40:19.530
and actually scale it to where
we can really just hammer

00:40:19.530 --> 00:40:21.940
away and parse
that stuff at scale

00:40:21.940 --> 00:40:23.660
and not to worry about
throwing 20 nodes

00:40:23.660 --> 00:40:27.880
at it allows us to create
this type of visibility

00:40:27.880 --> 00:40:30.750
into the environment where
we can say, explicitly,

00:40:30.750 --> 00:40:34.400
on any system that contains
third party data, whether it be

00:40:34.400 --> 00:40:36.970
regulated or otherwise,
these are the programs that

00:40:36.970 --> 00:40:37.980
were added and removed.

00:40:37.980 --> 00:40:39.370
These are the users that did it.

00:40:39.370 --> 00:40:41.540
This is the time frame.

00:40:41.540 --> 00:40:44.470
These are the security tools
that may have been turned off.

00:40:44.470 --> 00:40:46.780
And to do all that
without agents,

00:40:46.780 --> 00:40:51.110
especially on some of the older
systems, is absolutely vital.

00:40:51.110 --> 00:40:54.632
Now having said that, if they
do have agents out there,

00:40:54.632 --> 00:40:57.090
we want to be able to take full
advantage of those as well.

00:40:57.090 --> 00:40:59.180
And we do have-- we've
spent a lot of time

00:40:59.180 --> 00:41:00.680
because we think
it's really, really

00:41:00.680 --> 00:41:03.300
valuable to be able to push
and pull from those agents.

00:41:03.300 --> 00:41:06.750
So if we want to take all
the data they're seeing,

00:41:06.750 --> 00:41:10.520
pull it into dashboard and do
the analytics, but then also

00:41:10.520 --> 00:41:13.180
we want our analysts to be
able to pivot on that data

00:41:13.180 --> 00:41:15.270
and interrogate the
endpoints themselves.

00:41:15.270 --> 00:41:17.830
So if they see
something in this where

00:41:17.830 --> 00:41:19.910
they may want to
do a second level

00:41:19.910 --> 00:41:22.000
and actually pull a hash
value that gives them

00:41:22.000 --> 00:41:24.540
a fingerprint of one
of these programs,

00:41:24.540 --> 00:41:26.060
we want them to
be able to do that

00:41:26.060 --> 00:41:27.670
without having to
leave the ecosystem

00:41:27.670 --> 00:41:31.160
and pull up yet
another pane of glass.

00:41:31.160 --> 00:41:34.057
And this is really all about
joining this stuff together.

00:41:38.400 --> 00:41:42.200
Lastly, we've got to have
eye candy at some level.

00:41:42.200 --> 00:41:45.580
And this is that eye candy.

00:41:45.580 --> 00:41:48.990
But it does actually demonstrate
a really interesting point,

00:41:48.990 --> 00:41:53.430
which is we're able to,
as the data comes in,

00:41:53.430 --> 00:41:56.420
fully geolocate it, do
all the anomaly detection,

00:41:56.420 --> 00:41:58.890
do all of that kind of stuff,
map it onto this visual,

00:41:58.890 --> 00:42:01.060
create this really kind
of interesting soft visual

00:42:01.060 --> 00:42:05.960
that while it looks pretty
and all those types of things,

00:42:05.960 --> 00:42:07.840
can actually convey
information as well.

00:42:07.840 --> 00:42:09.466
Because all of a
sudden if you see this

00:42:09.466 --> 00:42:11.798
and then you see a massive
connection going to a country

00:42:11.798 --> 00:42:13.470
you know you don't
do business with,

00:42:13.470 --> 00:42:15.320
that should fire up an alert.

00:42:15.320 --> 00:42:17.370
Somebody should
go run that down.

00:42:17.370 --> 00:42:19.800
And so it's really
all about creating

00:42:19.800 --> 00:42:21.830
these kind of novel
and interesting ways

00:42:21.830 --> 00:42:23.200
to look at the data.

00:42:25.494 --> 00:42:28.160
That may have been possible with
certain types of infrastructure

00:42:28.160 --> 00:42:31.050
before, but we really
want to be able to do that

00:42:31.050 --> 00:42:33.985
across the board, and do that
in a cost effective manner,

00:42:33.985 --> 00:42:36.110
and do it in a manner that
helps organizations best

00:42:36.110 --> 00:42:37.040
protect themselves.

00:42:37.040 --> 00:42:38.540
And that's really
what we're focused

00:42:38.540 --> 00:42:42.310
on when we talk about our
security analytic solutions

00:42:42.310 --> 00:42:44.630
and how we're helping
our clients kind of move

00:42:44.630 --> 00:42:47.190
through some of
these problem sets.

00:42:47.190 --> 00:42:50.910
So with that, I believe
we have finished with kind

00:42:50.910 --> 00:42:52.440
of two minutes to spare.

00:42:52.440 --> 00:42:55.207
So I want to say thank
you, everyone for listening

00:42:55.207 --> 00:42:56.290
to the presentation today.

00:42:56.290 --> 00:42:59.300
If you have any questions,
we'll be around up here

00:42:59.300 --> 00:43:00.160
after the talk.

00:43:00.160 --> 00:43:02.880
Otherwise, thank you for
hanging out for the last slot.

00:43:02.880 --> 00:43:04.430
And have a great one.

00:43:04.430 --> 00:43:07.720
[APPLAUSE]

